  "body": {
    "id": "thm:all-regime-bracket",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Uniformly over \\(n\\ge1\\) and \\(d\\ge2\\), \\(c_\\epsilon L_{n,d}\\le R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\le C_\\epsilon U_{n,d}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(n\\)",
      "\\(d\\)",
      "\\(\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(c_\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(L_{n,d}\\)",
      "\\(R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\)",
      "\\(C_\\epsilon\\)",
      "\\(U_{n,d}\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:benchmarks",
      "def:minimax-risk",
      "thm:empirical-upper",
      "thm:separated-atom-lower",
      "thm:scalar-transfer-lower"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem combines complementary same-class lower obstructions with the total empirical upper bound; its explicit benchmarks imply the uniform at-most-\\(\\log(ed)\\) characterization.",
    "gap": "The raw bracket is sharp in the dense branch but leaves an at-most-logarithmic factor in the sparse and transitional regimes.",
    "consumer": "The bounds provide valid sample-size diagnostics on the full observational class before the exact frontier is known.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(n\\geq 1\\), \\(d\\geq 2\\), and \\(0<\\epsilon<1/2\\).  By \\(\\mathrm{thm:separated\\mbox{-}atom\\mbox{-}lower}\\) and \\(\\mathrm{thm:scalar\\mbox{-}transfer\\mbox{-}lower}\\), there are constants \\(c_{1,\\epsilon}>0\\) and \\(c_{2,\\epsilon}>0\\), depending only on \\(\\epsilon\\), for which\n\\[\n R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\geq c_{1,\\epsilon}\\min\\!\\left\\{\\frac dn,n^{-2/3}\\right\\},\n \\qquad\n R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\geq c_{2,\\epsilon}\\min\\!\\left\\{1,\\frac{d}{n\\log(ed)}\\right\\}.\n\\]\nIf \\(r\\geq c_1 a\\) and \\(r\\geq c_2 b\\) for nonnegative \\(a,b,r\\) and positive \\(c_1,c_2\\), then\n\\[\n r\\geq \\min\\{c_1,c_2\\}\\max\\{a,b\\}.\n\\]\nApplying this elementary implication and then \\(\\mathrm{def:benchmarks}\\) gives\n\\[\n R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\geq\n \\min\\{c_{1,\\epsilon},c_{2,\\epsilon}\\}\n \\max\\!\\left\\{\n   \\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac{d}{n\\log(ed)}\\right),\n   \\min\\!\\left(\\frac dn,n^{-2/3}\\right)\n \\right\\}\n =c_\\epsilon L_{n,d},\n\\]\nafter setting \\(c_\\epsilon=\\min\\{c_{1,\\epsilon},c_{2,\\epsilon}\\}>0\\).\n\nFor the upper bound, \\(\\widehat G_{\\mathrm{emp}}\\) is, by \\(\\mathrm{thm:empirical\\mbox{-}upper}\\), a measurable \\(\\mathcal P([-1,1])\\)-valued statistic.  It is therefore an admissible decision rule in the infimum defining \\(R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\\) in \\(\\mathrm{def:minimax\\mbox{-}risk}\\).  Hence\n\\[\n \\begin{aligned}\n R_{n,d,\\epsilon}\n &\\leq \\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal M_{d,\\epsilon}}\n E_{P^n}\\!\\left[W_1(\\widehat G_{\\mathrm{emp}},G_P)^2\\right] \\\\\n &\\leq C_\\epsilon\\min\\!\\left(1,\\frac dn\\right)\n =C_\\epsilon U_{n,d},\n \\end{aligned}\n\\]\nwhere the second inequality is \\(\\mathrm{thm:empirical\\mbox{-}upper}\\) and the equality is \\(\\mathrm{def:benchmarks}\\).  All constants depend only on the fixed overlap constant \\(\\epsilon\\), so both inequalities hold uniformly over the stated \\(n\\) and \\(d\\).",
